# 7.6 CSS Syntax and Selectors

> Written for SHSID from Indexademics (IDX) study guides. Original material by the IDX club; please keep this credit on any copy.

## CSS syntax

CSS is used to control the presentation of HTML elements, including layout, colours, fonts and more. A CSS rule consists of a selector and a declaration block.

```css
selector {
    property: value;
    property: value;
    /* more declarations */
}
```

| Part | Definition |
|---|---|
| **Selector** | Targets the HTML element or elements to be styled |
| **Declaration block** | Enclosed in curly braces `{}`; contains one or more declarations |
| **Declaration** | Consists of a CSS property and a value, separated by a colon `:`; each declaration ends with a semicolon `;` |

## Element selectors

An element selector selects all HTML elements of a given type.

```css
p {
    color: #333;            /* Dark gray color */
    font-family: sans-serif;
    line-height: 1.6;
}
```

This styles all `<p>` elements to be dark grey, in a sans-serif font, with a line height of 1.6.

## Class selectors

A class selector selects elements with a specific `class` attribute. Class selectors start with a dot.

```css
.highlight-text {
    background-color: yellow;
    padding: 5px;
    font-weight: bold;
}
```

This styles elements with `class="highlight-text"` to have a yellow background, padding and bold font.

## ID selectors

An ID selector selects the element with a specific `id` attribute. ID selectors start with a hash `#`. IDs should be unique, which is why an ID selector normally matches exactly one element while a class selector may match many.

```css
#main-navigation {
    background-color: #f9f9f9;
    border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd;
    padding: 10px 0;
}
```

## Universal selector

The universal selector selects all HTML elements, and is represented by an asterisk `*`.

```css
* {
    box-sizing: border-box;   /* Include padding and border in element's total width and height */
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
}
```

This resets margin and padding for all elements and sets `box-sizing` to `border-box`.

## Attribute selectors

Attribute selectors select elements based on the presence or value of an attribute.

```css
input[type="text"] {
    border: 1px solid #ccc;
    border-radius: 3px;
    padding: 8px;
}

a[target="_blank"] {   /* Selects links with target="_blank" */
    color: blue;
}
```

## Pseudo-class selectors

Pseudo-classes select elements based on state or position, and start with a single colon `:`.

```css
a:hover {              /* Style links on mouse hover */
    color: red;
    text-decoration: none;
}

button:focus {         /* Style button when focused (e.g. after clicking) */
    outline: 2px solid blue;
}

/* Structural pseudo-classes */
li:first-child {       /* First list item */
    font-weight: bold;
}

li:last-child {        /* Last list item */
    border-bottom: none;
}

p:nth-child(odd) {     /* Every odd paragraph */
    background-color: #f0f0f0;
}

p:nth-child(even) {    /* Every even paragraph */
    background-color: #e0e0e0;
}

/* UI state pseudo-classes */
input:enabled {        /* Styles enabled input fields */
    border-color: green;
}

input:disabled {       /* Styles disabled input fields */
    background-color: #ddd;
    color: #999;
}

input:checked + label {  /* Styles label next to a checked checkbox */
    font-weight: bold;
}
```

## Pseudo-element selectors

Pseudo-elements select specific parts of an element, and start with a double colon `::`. The number of colons is the whole distinction between the two families: one colon for a state or position, two for a piece of the element.

```css
p::first-line {        /* Style the first line of paragraphs */
    font-weight: bold;
}

ul::before {           /* Insert content before unordered lists */
    content: "List: ";
    font-weight: bold;
}

/* ::marker - styles the bullet or number of a list item */
li::marker {
    color: blue;
    font-size: 1.2em;
}

/* ::selection - styles the text that is selected by the user */
::selection {
    background-color: #ffcc80;   /* Light orange */
    color: white;
}

/* ::placeholder - styles the placeholder text in input fields */
input::placeholder {
    color: #aaa;
    font-style: italic;
}
```

## Combinators

Combinators define the relationship between selectors.

| Combinator | Written | Selects |
|---|---|---|
| **Descendant** | `div p` | All `<p>` elements inside `<div>` elements |
| **Child** | `ul > li` | Only direct `<li>` children of `<ul>` |
| **Adjacent sibling** | `h2 + p` | The first `<p>` immediately after an `<h2>` |
| **General sibling** | `div ~ p` | All `<p>` siblings of a `<div>` that come after the div |

```css
div p { color: green; }
ul > li { list-style-type: circle; }
h2 + p { margin-top: 0; }
div ~ p { font-size: 0.9em; }
```

## Keywords

| Term | Definition |
|---|---|
| **Selector** | Targets the HTML element or elements to be styled |
| **Declaration block** | Enclosed in curly braces, containing one or more declarations |
| **Declaration** | A CSS property and a value separated by a colon, ended with a semicolon |
| **Element selector** | Selects all HTML elements of a given type |
| **Class selector** | Selects elements with a specific class attribute; starts with a dot |
| **ID selector** | Selects the element with a specific id attribute; starts with a hash |
| **Universal selector** | Selects all HTML elements; represented by an asterisk `*` |
| **Attribute selector** | Selects elements based on the presence or value of an attribute |
| **Pseudo-class** | Selects elements based on state or position; starts with a colon |
| **Pseudo-element** | Selects specific parts of an element; starts with a double colon |
| **Combinator** | Defines the relationship between selectors |

# 7.8 Styling Lists, Tables and Media Queries

> Written for SHSID from Indexademics (IDX) study guides. Original material by the IDX club; please keep this credit on any copy.

## List styling

`list-style-type` sets the bullet type for `<ul>` and the numbering for `<ol>`. Its values include `disc`, `circle`, `square`, `decimal`, `decimal-leading-zero`, `lower-roman`, `upper-roman`, `lower-alpha`, `upper-alpha` and `none`.

`list-style-position` sets the marker position, either `inside` or `outside`. `outside` is the default, with markers outside the content box; `inside` places markers inside, affecting text flow — which is why the example below adds padding to compensate.

```css
ul {
    list-style-type: square;      /* Square bullets */
}

ol {
    list-style-type: upper-roman; /* Uppercase Roman numerals */
}

.custom-list {
    list-style-image: url('images/checkmark.png');  /* Custom image bullet */
    list-style-position: inside;
    padding-left: 20px;           /* Adjust padding for inside position */
}
```

## Table styling

`border-collapse` takes `collapse`, giving a single border, or `separate`, giving separated borders. `collapse` is often preferred for cleaner table designs, because adjacent cells then share one border line rather than each drawing its own.

`table-layout` sets the algorithm used to lay out table cells, rows and columns, either `auto` or `fixed`. `fixed` can be faster for rendering large tables.

```css
table {
    border-collapse: collapse;   /* Single borders */
    width: 100%;
}

th, td {
    border: 1px solid #ddd;
    padding: 8px;
    text-align: left;
}

th {
    background-color: #f0f0f0;   /* Light gray header background */
    font-weight: bold;
}

tbody tr:nth-child(even) {       /* Zebra striping for even rows */
    background-color: #f9f9f9;
}

caption {
    caption-side: bottom;        /* Caption below table */
    font-style: italic;
    color: gray;
    padding-top: 10px;
}
```

Note how the zebra striping reuses the `:nth-child(even)` pseudo-class from the selectors lesson: no extra classes are needed in the HTML, because the rule picks out alternate rows by position.

## Responsive design with media queries

**Media queries** apply different styles based on device characteristics, such as screen width. A media query is written with `@media` followed by a media type — `screen`, `print` or `all` — and conditions such as `max-width`, `min-width` or `orientation`.

```css
/* For screens smaller than 768px wide (e.g. mobile devices) */
@media screen and (max-width: 768px) {
    body {
        font-size: 14px;    /* Smaller font size for mobile */
    }
    .container {
        width: 100%;        /* Full width container on mobile */
    }
}

/* For screens 768px wide and larger (e.g. desktops) */
@media screen and (min-width: 769px) {
    body {
        font-size: 16px;    /* Default font size for desktop */
    }
    .container {
        width: 960px;       /* Fixed width container on desktop */
        margin: 0 auto;     /* Center container on desktop */
    }
}
```

The two blocks are written as a matched pair, with `max-width: 768px` and `min-width: 769px` meeting exactly at the boundary so that every screen width falls into one block and no width falls into both.

Orientation can be tested in the same way:

```css
@media screen and (orientation: portrait) {
    /* Styles for portrait mode (e.g. mobile phone vertically) */
    .menu {
        flex-direction: column;   /* Stack menu items vertically in portrait */
    }
}

@media screen and (orientation: landscape) {
    /* Styles for landscape mode (e.g. mobile phone horizontally, tablet, desktop) */
    .menu {
        flex-direction: row;      /* Display menu items horizontally in landscape */
    }
}
```

Device pixel ratio can be used to serve higher-resolution assets to Retina screens:

```css
@media (-webkit-min-device-pixel-ratio: 2), (min-resolution: 192dpi) {
    .logo {
        content: url(logo-2x.png);  /* Use a higher resolution logo for Retina screens */
        width: 200px;               /* Adjust width as needed */
    }
}
```

Media queries can also use features such as `device-width`, `device-height`, `aspect-ratio`, `color` and `resolution`. They can be combined using logical operators like `and`, `or` and `not`, and can target different media types — for example `@media print { /* print styles here */ }`.

## Keywords

| Term | Definition |
|---|---|
| **`list-style-type`** | Bullet type for `<ul>` and numbering for `<ol>` |
| **`list-style-position`** | Marker position: `outside` (default, markers outside the content box) or `inside` (markers inside, affecting text flow) |
| **`list-style-image`** | Uses a custom image as the list bullet |
| **`border-collapse`** | `collapse` gives a single border, `separate` gives separated borders |
| **`table-layout`** | The algorithm used to lay out table cells, rows and columns: `auto` or `fixed` |
| **`caption-side`** | Places the table caption, for example below the table |
| **Media query** | Applies different styles based on device characteristics such as screen width, written with `@media` |
| **`max-width` / `min-width`** | Conditions setting the upper and lower screen width a media query applies to |
| **`orientation`** | Media query condition distinguishing portrait from landscape |

# 18 CSS: Syntax and Selectors

> Written for SHSID from Indexademics (IDX) study guides. Original material by the IDX club; please keep this credit on any copy.

**CSS** (Cascading Style Sheets) is used to control the presentation of HTML elements, including layout, colours, fonts and more. Where HTML structures the content of a webpage, CSS styles and visually formats that content.

## CSS syntax

CSS rules consist of a selector and a declaration block.

```css
selector {
    property: value;
    property: value;
    /* more declarations */
}
```

- The **selector** targets the HTML element or elements to be styled.
- The **declaration block** is enclosed in curly braces `{}` and contains one or more declarations.
- A **declaration** consists of a CSS property and a value, separated by a colon `:`, and each declaration ends with a semicolon `;`.

## Element selectors

An element selector selects all HTML elements of a given type.

```css
p {
    color: #333;          /* Dark gray color */
    font-family: sans-serif;
    line-height: 1.6;
}
```

This styles all `<p>` elements to be dark grey, in a sans-serif font, with a line height of 1.6.

## Class selectors

A class selector selects elements with a specific `class` attribute. Class selectors start with a dot `.`.

```css
.highlight-text {
    background-color: yellow;
    padding: 5px;
    font-weight: bold;
}
```

This styles elements with `class="highlight-text"` to have a yellow background, padding and bold font.

## ID selectors

An ID selector selects the element with a specific `id` attribute. ID selectors start with a hash `#`. IDs should be unique.

```css
#main-navigation {
    background-color: #f9f9f9;
    border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd;
    padding: 10px 0;
}
```

The difference between class and ID follows from that uniqueness rule: a class describes a kind of element and may be reused across the page, while an ID names one particular element.

## Universal selector

The universal selector selects all HTML elements. It is represented by an asterisk `*`.

```css
* {
    box-sizing: border-box;   /* Include padding and border in element's total width and height */
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
}
```

This resets margin and padding for all elements and sets `box-sizing` to `border-box`.

## Attribute selectors

An attribute selector selects elements based on the presence or value of an attribute.

```css
input[type="text"] {
    border: 1px solid #ccc;
    border-radius: 3px;
    padding: 8px;
}

a[target="_blank"] {   /* Selects links with target="_blank" */
    color: blue;
}
```

## Pseudo-class selectors

A **pseudo-class** selects elements based on state or position. Pseudo-classes start with a single colon `:`.

```css
a:hover {              /* Style links on mouse hover */
    color: red;
    text-decoration: none;
}

button:focus {         /* Style button when focused (e.g., after clicking) */
    outline: 2px solid blue;
}

/* Structural pseudo-classes */
li:first-child {       /* First list item */
    font-weight: bold;
}

li:last-child {        /* Last list item */
    border-bottom: none;
}

p:nth-child(odd) {     /* Every odd paragraph */
    background-color: #f0f0f0;
}

p:nth-child(even) {    /* Every even paragraph */
    background-color: #e0e0e0;
}

/* UI state pseudo-classes */
input:enabled {        /* Styles enabled input fields */
    border-color: green;
}

input:disabled {       /* Styles disabled input fields */
    background-color: #ddd;
    color: #999;
}

input:checked + label { /* Styles label next to a checked checkbox */
    font-weight: bold;
}
```

These fall into three groups: state pseudo-classes such as `:hover` and `:focus`, structural pseudo-classes such as `:first-child` and `:nth-child()`, and UI state pseudo-classes such as `:enabled`, `:disabled` and `:checked`. In every case the style depends on something the HTML does not say directly.

## Pseudo-element selectors

A **pseudo-element** selects a specific part of an element. Pseudo-elements start with a double colon `::`.

```css
p::first-line {        /* Style the first line of paragraphs */
    font-weight: bold;
}

ul::before {           /* Insert content before unordered lists */
    content: "List: ";
    font-weight: bold;
}

li::marker {           /* Styles the bullet or number of a list item */
    color: blue;
    font-size: 1.2em;
}

::selection {          /* Styles the text that is selected by the user */
    background-color: #ffcc80;   /* Light orange */
    color: white;
}

input::placeholder {   /* Styles the placeholder text in input fields */
    color: #aaa;
    font-style: italic;
}
```

The single colon and double colon are not interchangeable: one colon selects a whole element in some state, two colons select a part of an element that has no tag of its own.

## Combinators

**Combinators** define the relationship between selectors.

```css
/* Descendant selector */
div p {        /* Selects all <p> elements inside <div> elements */
    color: green;
}

/* Child selector */
ul > li {      /* Selects only direct <li> children of <ul> */
    list-style-type: circle;
}

/* Adjacent sibling selector */
h2 + p {       /* Selects the first <p> immediately after an <h2> */
    margin-top: 0;
}

/* General sibling selector */
div ~ p {      /* Selects all <p> siblings of a <div> that come after the div */
    font-size: 0.9em;
}
```

| Combinator | Symbol | Selects |
|---|---|---|
| Descendant | space | Elements anywhere inside another element |
| Child | `>` | Only direct children |
| Adjacent sibling | `+` | The first matching sibling immediately after |
| General sibling | `~` | All matching siblings that come after |

Descendant and child differ by depth; adjacent and general sibling differ by how many siblings are matched.

## Three ways to include CSS in HTML

There are three main ways to incorporate CSS into HTML documents.

### Inline CSS

Applying styles directly within HTML tags using the `style` attribute.

```html
<p style="color: green; font-size: 16px;">This paragraph is styled inline.</p>
```

**Pros:** quick for testing or very specific, one-off styles.
**Cons:** not maintainable for larger projects, difficult to reuse styles, mixes content with presentation. Generally discouraged for extensive styling.

### Internal CSS (embedded CSS)

CSS rules are defined within the `<style>` tag inside the `<head>` section of the HTML document.

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
<title>Internal CSS Example</title>
<style>
    p {
        color: blue;
        font-size: 16px;
    }
</style>
</head>
<body>
    <p>This paragraph is styled using internal CSS.</p>
</body>
</html>
```

**Pros:** useful for page-specific styles; keeps CSS and HTML in one file, which can be convenient for single-page documents.
**Cons:** less maintainable than external stylesheets for larger sites, and still mixes structure and presentation to some extent.

### External CSS (linked CSS)

CSS rules are written in a separate `.css` file and linked to the HTML document using the `<link>` tag in the `<head>` section. This is the recommended method for most projects.

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
    <title>External CSS Example</title>
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="styles.css">
</head>
<body>
    <p>This paragraph is styled using external CSS.</p>
</body>
</html>
```

```css
p {
    color: blue;
    font-size: 16px;
}
```

**Pros:** best for maintainability, reusability and organisation; a clear separation of content (HTML) and presentation (CSS); easier to update styles across a website; improves website performance through caching of CSS files.
**Cons:** requires managing separate files.

The three methods form a scale from most local to most shared, and the recommendation follows the same scale: the further the style is pulled out of the markup, the more elements it can serve and the fewer places you have to edit when it changes.

## Keywords

| Term | Definition |
|---|---|
| **CSS** | Cascading Style Sheets, used to control the presentation of HTML elements including layout, colours and fonts |
| **Selector** | The part of a CSS rule that targets the HTML element(s) to be styled |
| **Declaration block** | The part of a CSS rule enclosed in curly braces, containing one or more declarations |
| **Declaration** | A CSS property and a value separated by a colon, ending with a semicolon |
| **Element selector** | Selects all HTML elements of a given type |
| **Class selector** | Selects elements with a specific `class` attribute; starts with a dot `.` |
| **ID selector** | Selects the element with a specific `id` attribute; starts with a hash `#`; IDs should be unique |
| **Universal selector** | Selects all HTML elements; represented by an asterisk `*` |
| **Attribute selector** | Selects elements based on the presence or value of an attribute |
| **Pseudo-class** | Selects elements based on state or position; starts with a single colon `:` |
| **Pseudo-element** | Selects specific parts of an element; starts with a double colon `::` |
| **Combinator** | A symbol defining the relationship between selectors: descendant (space), child (`>`), adjacent sibling (`+`), general sibling (`~`) |
| **Inline CSS** | Styles applied directly within HTML tags using the `style` attribute |
| **Internal CSS** | CSS rules defined within a `<style>` tag inside the `<head>` section |
| **External CSS** | CSS rules written in a separate `.css` file and linked with `<link>`; the recommended method for most projects |

# 19 CSS: Properties, Units and Responsive Design

> Written for SHSID from Indexademics (IDX) study guides. Original material by the IDX club; please keep this credit on any copy.

CSS properties control various aspects of element styling. This lesson covers the property groups, the units their values are written in, and the mechanism that lets one stylesheet serve different screen sizes.

## Text and background properties

| Property | Controls | Example |
|---|---|---|
| `color` | Text colour | `color: red;` |
| `text-decoration` | Underline, overline and similar | `text-decoration: underline;` |
| `background-color` | Background colour | `background-color: #eee;` |

Note that `color` sets the colour of the text itself, while `background-color` sets the colour behind it.

## The box model

The CSS **box model** describes the rectangular boxes that represent HTML elements.

| Property | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `margin` | Space **outside** the element's border. Also `margin-top`, `margin-right`, `margin-bottom`, `margin-left`. Can use `auto` for horizontal centring |
| `padding` | Space **inside** the element's border, between border and content. Also `padding-top`, `padding-right`, `padding-bottom`, `padding-left` |
| `border` | The element's border, e.g. `border: 1px solid black;`. Related properties are `border-width`, `border-style` (`solid`, `dashed`, `dotted`, `double`), `border-color`, `border-radius` for rounded corners, and individual sides such as `border-top` |
| `box-sizing` | How width and height are calculated. `border-box` includes padding and border in the width and height; `content-box` is the default, where width and height apply only to the content |
| `overflow` | Handles content overflow: `hidden`, `scroll`, `auto`, `visible`. Also `overflow-x` and `overflow-y` |
| `visibility` | Controls element visibility: `hidden` hides the element but it still takes up space, `visible`, `collapse` |

Margin and padding are the same kind of quantity on opposite sides of the border, which is why they take the same set of sub-properties. The `box-sizing` choice matters because under the default `content-box`, adding padding makes an element wider than the width you set; `border-box` folds the padding and border back inside the stated width.

The note on `visibility: hidden` is worth keeping: the element disappears but its space does not, so the layout around it does not close up.

## Display properties

Display properties control how elements are displayed and laid out.

## Positioning properties

Positioning properties control the position of elements within the document.

| Property | Effect |
|---|---|
| `position: relative;` | Position relative to its normal position. Use `top`, `right`, `bottom`, `left` to offset |
| `position: absolute;` | Removed from document flow, positioned relative to the nearest positioned ancestor (or `<html>`) |
| `top`, `right`, `bottom`, `left` | Offsets for positioned elements. Can use pixel values, percentages or other CSS units |
| `z-index` | Stack order of elements; a higher value is in front. Only works on positioned elements (relative, absolute, fixed, sticky) |
| `float` | Positions an element to the left or right and allows text and inline elements to wrap around it: `float: left;`, `float: right;`, `float: none;` |
| `clear` | Specifies which sides of an element are not allowed to float next to other floating elements: `clear: both;`, `clear: left;`, `clear: right;`, `clear: none;` |

The restriction on `z-index` follows from what it does: stacking order only means something once an element has been taken out of the ordinary flow, so `z-index` has no effect on an unpositioned element.

`float` was often used for layout, but Flexbox and Grid are generally preferred for modern layouts. `clear` exists to control the flow around floated elements.

## CSS units

Units in CSS define sizes and lengths, and they divide into two kinds.

**Absolute units** are fixed sizes, not relative to anything else.

| Unit | Notes |
|---|---|
| `px` (pixels) | The basic unit, device-dependent. Generally recommended for screen sizes |
| `pt`, `pc`, `in`, `cm`, `mm` | Points, picas, inches, centimetres, millimetres. Physical units, less common for screen display and more relevant for print styles |

**Relative units** are sizes relative to other values.

| Unit | Relative to |
|---|---|
| `%` | The parent element or viewport. Very versatile for responsive design |
| `em` | The font size of the parent element; `1em` is the current font size. Useful for maintaining proportional scaling relative to text size |
| `rem` (root em) | The font size of the root element `<html>`. Good for consistent scaling across the entire website based on a single root font size |
| `vw` (viewport width) | The width of the viewport |
| `vh` (viewport height) | The height of the viewport |
| `vmin`, `vmax` | The smaller or larger dimension of the viewport. Useful for elements that need to scale based on the viewport's smallest or largest dimension |

The distinction between `em` and `rem` is the source of most confusion here: `em` compounds, because it looks to the parent, while `rem` always looks to the same root value, which is why `rem` gives consistent scaling across a whole site.

## Styling lists

| Property | Controls |
|---|---|
| `list-style-type` | Bullet type for `<ul>` and numbering for `<ol>`: `disc`, `circle`, `square`, `decimal`, `decimal-leading-zero`, `lower-roman`, `upper-roman`, `lower-alpha`, `upper-alpha`, `none` |
| `list-style-position` | Marker position: `inside` or `outside`. `outside` is the default, with markers outside the content box; `inside` places markers inside, affecting text flow |

```css
ul {
    list-style-type: square;   /* Square bullets */
}

ol {
    list-style-type: upper-roman;   /* Uppercase Roman numerals */
}

.custom-list {
    list-style-image: url('images/checkmark.png');  /* Custom image bullet */
    list-style-position: inside;
    padding-left: 20px;   /* Adjust padding for inside position */
}
```

## Styling tables

| Property | Controls |
|---|---|
| `border-collapse` | `collapse` gives a single border, `separate` gives separated borders. `collapse` is often preferred for cleaner table designs |
| `table-layout` | The algorithm used to lay out table cells, rows and columns: `auto` or `fixed`. `fixed` can be faster for rendering large tables |

```css
table {
    border-collapse: collapse;   /* Single borders */
    width: 100%;
}

th, td {
    border: 1px solid #ddd;
    padding: 8px;
    text-align: left;
}

th {
    background-color: #f0f0f0;   /* Light gray header background */
    font-weight: bold;
}

tbody tr:nth-child(even) {       /* Zebra striping for even rows */
    background-color: #f9f9f9;
}

caption {
    caption-side: bottom;        /* Caption below table */
    font-style: italic;
    color: gray;
    padding-top: 10px;
}
```

This example brings the previous lesson's selectors back into use: the element selector styles `th` and `td` together, and the `:nth-child(even)` pseudo-class produces zebra striping without any extra markup in the HTML.

## Responsive design with media queries

**Media queries** apply different styles based on device characteristics, such as screen width.

```css
/* For screens smaller than 768px wide (e.g., mobile devices) */
@media screen and (max-width: 768px) {
    body {
        font-size: 14px;    /* Smaller font size for mobile */
    }
    .container {
        width: 100%;        /* Full width container on mobile */
    }
}

/* For screens 768px wide and larger (e.g., desktops) */
@media screen and (min-width: 769px) {
    body {
        font-size: 16px;    /* Default font size for desktop */
    }
    .container {
        width: 960px;       /* Fixed width container on desktop */
        margin: 0 auto;     /* Center container on desktop */
    }
}

/* Example using orientation */
@media screen and (orientation: portrait) {
    .menu {
        flex-direction: column;   /* Stack menu items vertically in portrait */
    }
}

@media screen and (orientation: landscape) {
    .menu {
        flex-direction: row;      /* Display menu items horizontally in landscape */
    }
}

/* Example using device pixel ratio for high-resolution screens (Retina) */
@media (-webkit-min-device-pixel-ratio: 2), (min-resolution: 192dpi) {
    .logo {
        content: url(logo-2x.png);  /* Use a higher resolution logo for Retina screens */
        width: 200px;
    }
}
```

Media queries use `@media` followed by a media type — `screen`, `print` or `all` — and conditions such as `max-width`, `min-width` and `orientation`. They can also use features like `device-width`, `device-height`, `aspect-ratio`, `color` and `resolution`. Media queries can be combined using logical operators such as `and`, `or` and `not`, and can target different media types, for example `@media print { /* print styles here */ }`.

Note the breakpoints in the first two examples: `max-width: 768px` and `min-width: 769px`. They are one pixel apart so that no screen width satisfies both, which is what stops the two blocks from fighting over the same device.

## Keywords

| Term | Definition |
|---|---|
| **Box model** | The CSS model describing the rectangular boxes that represent HTML elements |
| **margin** | Space outside the element's border |
| **padding** | Space inside the element's border, between border and content |
| **box-sizing** | How width and height are calculated; `border-box` includes padding and border, `content-box` (the default) applies width and height only to the content |
| **overflow** | Property handling content that does not fit: `hidden`, `scroll`, `auto`, `visible` |
| **visibility** | Property controlling element visibility; `hidden` hides the element but it still takes up space |
| **position: relative** | Positions an element relative to its normal position |
| **position: absolute** | Removes an element from document flow and positions it relative to the nearest positioned ancestor |
| **z-index** | The stack order of elements; higher values are in front. Only works on positioned elements |
| **float** | Positions an element to the left or right and allows text and inline elements to wrap around it |
| **clear** | Specifies which sides of an element are not allowed to float next to other floating elements |
| **Absolute units** | Fixed sizes not relative to anything else, such as `px`, `pt`, `in`, `cm`, `mm` |
| **Relative units** | Sizes relative to other values, such as `%`, `em`, `rem`, `vw`, `vh`, `vmin`, `vmax` |
| **em** | A unit relative to the font size of the parent element |
| **rem** | A unit relative to the font size of the root element `<html>` |
| **list-style-type** | The bullet type for `<ul>` or numbering for `<ol>` |
| **border-collapse** | Whether table borders are drawn as a single border (`collapse`) or separated (`separate`) |
| **Media query** | A rule beginning `@media` that applies different styles based on device characteristics such as screen width |
